for  example there could be pfas  in your lc solvent so in that case you  want to have  a delay column and that column is placed  after the mixer but before the auto  sampler  and what it does is delay the pfos that  from the background  and separate it from the pfos that's  coming from your sample  so you would end up with two peaks like  this with the first peak  being the actual pfos peak and then the  second  peak being the delayed peak and if  you're doing targeted analysis this is  really simple you can just set your mrm  window so you don't even see the delay  peak  so it's just basically a problem free  way of  making sure you have a nice clean  background one thing to be cons  to always consider though is the delay  column does not account for any  contamination coming from the auto  sampler  it only helps with the pump and the  solvents and that's because the auto  sampler is after  the delay column so for b  so for um doing non-targeted analysis i  would  definitely recommend implementing the  whole kit  just because you don't want to be  identifying things in your from your  samples that are actually just coming  from your background  and here so once you have your lc set up  you still have to do  you know your sample prep and consider  the vials that i mentioned  i just want to give a little shout out  many people don't know that agilent  actually purchased a standards company  so we now  sell environmental reference materials  and that  includes some pfas standards that are  shown here on the slide  we also sell the the vials and the caps  that  are you know floral polymer free  so you won't impart any backgrounds  there  we also sell some of the solid phase  extraction cartridges that are used in  the methods that i'm going to be talking  about  we've covered the background removal  the sample prep and now let's briefly  talk about your analytical choices  for analysis if you're doing targeted  analysis  versus non-targeted that's kind of how  this slide is divided up  targeted analysis you would probably  want to use a triple quad  and we have three triple quads in our  portfolio  and they are arranged here as the  from the more routine and to the  ultimate sensitivity end  the ultivo is um our routine  quantification  um and then it's a stackable lcms so it  has a greatly reduced  footprint then you have the 6470 and  then the 64.95 for ultimate sensitivity  depending on what kind of sample prep  you're doing that would help you decide  which one of these would best suit your  needs  i'm happy to announce uh for the first  time at asms  2020 that the 6470 now has  vac shield for no vent capillary  cleaning  previously the ultivo and the 6495 had  that but i'm very excited to announce  that it's now  also available on the 647d  if you are doing non-targeted analysis  maybe a q-top would or a high-res  instrument  would be more what you need and we have  several q-tops in our portfolio  listed here i'm probably specifically  only going to talk about the  which is the newest addition to that  family  and it also has gate valve for no vent  capillary cleaning  now let's jump into some methods and  results for targeted water analysis  epa 533 is the newest really released  epa method for drinking water  and it has some differences from its  predecessor  epa 537.1 um  first it covers more analytes you have  25 analytes versus 18.  they by switching the cartridge from the  sdvb  in the 537.1 to the wax cartridge in the  533  they were able to capture some of the  smaller analytes and you can see that in  the chart  on the right side of the screen the top  pfba and pfpea  are shorter chain compounds so they  weren't actually in 537.1 but they are  in 533  by adding those compounds they did  delete some of the longer chains that  were in 537  are not in 533 you'll note those on the  on the right side of the blue chart  midway down  um another difference between the two  methods is 533  is isotope dilution while 537 just used  internal standards  so you will be purchasing a lot more  isotopically labeled compounds  to run 533 so it is a little bit of a  more expensive method  they also change the final extract from  96 methanol to 80 methanol  and that's really to try and improve the  chromatography of some of the shorter  chain compounds  it's it's doing a full pfas analyte list  is always compromising  between the long chains and the short  chains since they seem to like different  chromographic and like solution  conditions  another change for epa 533 is they  required now they require two ions for  reporting  while in 537.1 that was not required  this method has a requires a 250 times  concentration factor  so it's perfect for routine instruments  like the ultivo and the 6470  i'm going to give a little bit of  introduction into our old tivo  it is the first stackable triple quad so  it just  you know resides at the bottom of the lc  stack which greatly reduces the  footprint  in your lab and in order to make it  that's much smaller than some of our  benchtop models the whole  interior of it had to be re-engineered  and the whole goal while they did this  was to reduce the footprint but maintain  the robust  sensitive performance of our benchtop  models  and i've used the ultivo specifically  for 537 and i can say that it performs  very well for that  besides the five uh the ultivo the 6470b  would also work very well to do 533  um and as i mentioned earlier the newest  release of it will now have vac shield  which can reduce your  downtime for when you need to clean the  front end from 6 hours to 30 minutes  this is a really robust instrument that  is very popular  and the the new version is pretty much  the same except for the vac shield  so if you can expect to have the same  routine  rugged performance that the other one  gave this one  works great for routine but it also can  give you a little extra boost if you  want to do  research applications on it here i'm  showing a comparison  of the ultivo and the 6470 for epa 533  second lab validation data we had  two labs that use our instruments  participate as secondary lab  validation and they all calculated the  lowest concentration minimum reporting  level  using both the ultivo and the 6470.  this lcmrl value considers both accuracy  and precision in analytical measurement  so it's a very robust value  and  you can see that both the ultivo and the  6470 for the most part performed very  well  in comparison with the epa single lab  lcmrl  you'll note that most of the values here  are below  or at to or around two nanograms per  liter  and that's kind of important i've had  many conversations with  uh customers and you know no one's  really certain where these regulations  are going to end up  but two nanograms per liter seems to be  kind of  the consensus goal that i've heard so  you want to really be able to measure  down pretty low  so 533 specifically for drinking water  this one the epa 8327 is for  non-potable water which would include  surface water groundwater and wastewater  it's currently pending released by the  epa  and it's as written the  range that it looks at is 10 to 400  nanograms per liter  what's great about this method which  many people really enjoy is that it's a  dilute and shoot method so you would  just take  five ml of your of your sample water  dilute it with five mils of methanol  filter it add a little acid and then  you're ready to inject so  that's a very simple sample prep  procedure that  that many people appreciate so we did  participate  in the epa as a second lab validation  for this method for the epa  and for that we use the 6470  and we we got really good results that  i'm going to show you  well within the method range  but as i mentioned no one really knows  where these regulations are going to  end up so many people want to go lower  so i'll show you some results  that i developed on the 6495 using this  method  for the second lab validation 