 levels there were some that  were a little bit higher  only one that was six or less  however and in for comparison it's not  shown on this slide but the 6470  all of the levels were at 10 nanograms  per liter  and they all except for two compounds  and those were actually at 20 nanograms  per liter  so the 64.95 is really able to give you  that extra boost of sensitivity if you  require it  i mentioned the robustness that the 64  95 is able to maintain and that's kind  of what this slide is trying to  illustrate  now if you take a look at each of one of  these little charts  they represent the each point on the  chart represents that  a calibration point and in between each  of those dots we injected  10 wastewater samples and then we took  another calibration reading  this is really just to verify that your  calibration  area counts remain stable even though  you're introducing a lot of matrix into  the instrument  and they're not normalized by an  internal standard there  i normalized them by the average area of  that particular compound  and you can see the rsds actually next  in the parentheses next to the compound  name i'm only showing six examples here  and they're all around six percent rsd  rsd and that six percent is calculated  over  100 over 100 sample injections and a 36  hour batch  now some some of the longer chains did  have slightly higher  rsds they were all well below the method  standard which is  30 percent but the longer chains might  have  had a little bit higher than six but i  don't think that has anything to do with  the 64.95  it has more to do with them sitting in  that vial for a long time  you know during the course of these  repeated injections and this really long  work list  so it likely just indicates that the  vials needed to be vortexed  because they've been sitting so long and  a lot of times the longer chains have a  tendency to absorb to the  vial walls  so 8327 is a dilute and shoot method but  if you're willing to  do some 